when 2 says no surface describing the built rule may contradict it, and the plan's Decisions block says every \"render-only\" copy was corrected. One copy was missed. `.prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md` line 369, in the `plan status --json` contract, still says \"`built` is render-only (a commit naming the chunk or touching its deliverables)\". That is now false twice over: `critic_mode.short_plan_deferral`'s `read_built` reads it to ARM a gate, and a change fragment that is still the `plan new` stub no longer counts as touching a deliverable. This matters because api-contract.md is a `governed_by` artifact that plans reconcile against. The belief that `built` is render-only (\"a table tag can afford it\") is how #1072 accepted the stub being read as built, which caused this issue's Stop-gate symptom. A maintainer who trusts this line would make the same call again. Core rule \"A coverage, completeness or absence claim ('X now handles Y', 'no Y', 'all') holds only when its falsifying query returns nothing\": a `render-only` grep over `.prawduct/artifacts/` and `plugin/` returns this line. Scope: instance. Change the sentence to state the relation instead (for example: `built` certifies nothing, a gate may read it only to arm, and a fragment still the `plan new` stub touches nothing; see `plan_status.built_report`), and do not add history around it.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Short-plan Stop-gate fix is claimed in the change record but tested only one layer down", "recommendation": "The change record's third bullet, and the plan's mid-build decision, say the fix stops a two-chunk short plan's Stop gate (`critic_mode.short_plan_deferral` via `read_built` -> `plan_status.built_chunks`) from demanding the boundary review during Chunk 01. `TestAStubIsNoChunksWork.test_right_after_plan_new_no_chunk_is_built` pins the underlying answer (`built_report` empty after the stub commit). No test in `tests/test_short_plan_deferral.py` drives the gate itself with a committed stub and an open Chunk 01. The call path is unchanged and direct, so the behavior is covered transitively. A gate-level case (two-chunk plan, committed stub, Chunk 01 uncommitted -> no last-chunk block) would keep that claim pinned if `read_built` ever moves off `built_chunks`. Scope: instance.", "files": ["tests/test_plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "api-contract still defines `built` as render-only, without the stub exclusion", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (the class sweep missed one tree). This change redefined the `built` tag in two ways. It now leaves out an unwritten change-fragment stub (`plan_status._work_paths` in `built_report`). And its docstrings now record that a gate reads it to arm (`critic_mode.short_plan_deferral`'s `read_built`). The plan's mid-build decision says every \"render-only\" copy was corrected. `.prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md` line ~369 (the `plan status --json` contract) still says \"`built` is render-only (a commit naming the chunk or touching its deliverables)\". Why it broke: the sweep searched `plugin/` but not `.prawduct/artifacts/`. Consequence: a maintainer who reads the contract believes no gate reads `built` and changes its rule freely, which silently moves when a short plan's Stop gate arms. A `--json` consumer is also told that any commit touching a Deliverable makes a chunk built, but a stub-only touch no longer does. Remedy: make the parenthetical relational, e.g. \"`built` is `plan_status.built_chunks`' answer\", or state the stub exclusion and the arm-only gate reader. api-contract.md is a `governed_by` artifact. If its \"render-only\" clause is read as the chunk-status plan's decision rather than a description, record the departure beneath the clause with the owner's confirmation and leave the clause's text alone (learnings authoring: \"Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless is the amend-to-match-own-code tell\"). The un-archived `.prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-status.md` lines 62-64 (\"`built` is render-only ... no gate reads it (pinned by test)\") repeat the claim, but that plan is a record of its own decision and needs no edit.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Caller-side head_tree guard inverts the callee's fail-closed fallback", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `critic_consolidate._proposed_chunks` passes `head_tree=head_tree if gitstate.is_object_id(head_tree) else None`. To `plan_status.chunk_proposal`, `None` means 'read the working tree'. A malformed value that reaches the callee instead reads every fragment as unreadable, which names fewer chunks. The plan's own decision says the malformed case is guarded inside the public function so that it names fewer. The caller's guard therefore turns that fail-closed path into a working-tree read. Today it cannot be reached, because `begin_review` always passes a captured tree id (critic_consolidate.py ~3171/3218/3299/3310). That is why this is a NOTE. Simplest fix: pass `head_tree` through unguarded so that only the callee's guard decides. The `since` guard beside it predates this change and is out of scope here.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Two public names for one proposal rule; pointers still name the wrapper", "recommendation": "Scope: class (search `propose_chunks` across plugin/ and tests/). The proposal rule's docstring now lives on `plan_status.chunk_proposal`. `propose_chunks` is a one-line wrapper whose only production caller is `critic_mode.extension_deferral_owed` (chunk mode). Several places still point at the wrapper as the rule's home, so a reader lands one hop away from it: SKILL.md step 4, critic_mode.py docstrings ~1670/1727/1909, and the `test_reviewer_payload_budget.py` comments that call it 'the rule's one home'. After R-4, critic_mode ~1670's 'names every chunk built and open at its dispatch' is also loose: a chunk named only by an unscoped subject is no longer proposed. This is prose that nothing reads, so no edit is wanted for it. If the wrapper is ever revisited, the construction is to have the single caller read `chunk_proposal(...).chunks` and delete `propose_chunks`, so that one name owns the rule.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The whole-plan proposal now counts only scoped subjects, but the short-plan next-step message still tells builders to use an unscoped subject", "recommendation": "The contract change is R-4: when a plan declares `scope:`, the certifying proposal counts only subjects of the form `<type>(<scope>): ...`, while `built` (which arms the short-plan gate through `read_built`) keeps the fallback to every subject. The impact on the proposal's downstream consumers was swept for \"touches\", \"Deliverables path\" and \"every subject counts\", but not for prose that tells a builder how to write the subject. `critic_mode.short_plan_next_step`, which this diff did not touch, is rendered by inference and by both Stop-gate messages, so it reaches every consumer repo. It still says \"commit this chunk, naming it in the subject (`\u2026 (Chunk <id>)`)\" and says the boundary review \"names every chunk built and still open\". After this change neither holds for a path-less chunk on a scoped plan whose subjects lack the scope: inference sees it as built, the boundary `cumulative` leaves it out, and only the new dispatch NOTE points to `--chunks`. Its docstring makes the same claim (\"the boundary review names every open chunk that is built (`propose_chunks` in `cumulative` mode)